### ARTICLE ARCHIVE: Computer Games Strategy Plus (April 1995)

**Title:** MORTAL REALMS: J. Grant Bell zeroes in on the best of the MUDs
**Section:** OVER THE NET
**Author:** J. Grant Bell
**Issue:** No. 53 

There are literally hundreds of MUDs on the Internet. Some of them are long-term games with established user bases, interesting and unique game worlds, and dedicated programmers. Others are fly-by-night operations, resembling nothing more than crude temporary structures that will blow away at the slightest gust of wind. These MUDs appear and disappear in a matter of weeks. 

What makes a MUD successful? Variety and a good implementation of the game itself help a lot. Often, MUDs lack these qualities. Why? Fully operational versions of several basic types of MUD code are available to anyone who wants to download them from the internet. Often, people simply download the MUD code, put it on a University-owned computer, then sit back and watch. These people seem to view their MUDs as electronic ant farms: simply set them up and add water, and soon the adventurers will be crawling around in the MUD! 

Unfortunately, though, these MUDs are entirely bland and lacking in character. It takes a lot of work to transform basic MUD code into a unique and compelling experience, and few MUDs achieve this transformation. So just in case you're frustrated with the MUDs you've already tried, this month we'll take a look at Mortal Realms. It's a good place to start a MUDding career, and once you've started it's likely you'll be staying. 

### That Special Variety of MUD

So what makes MR so great, anyway? Aside from the actual game mechanics, this MUD has something that many MUDs do not have: an established, reliable site with relatively little Internet lag. Many good MUDs are rendered unplayable because they are constantly moved from location to location, or because they are off-line for extended periods of time. Still others suffer from extreme lag, so that your commands aren't executed until 30 or more seconds after they are typed (!). MR has a good, reliable location. It's found at hydrogen.ee.utulsa.edu 4321 or 129.244.42.48 4321. 

Another nice thing about MR is its game world. It has been totally overhauled, so that very few locations from the original code remain. This is a blessing for anyone who has adventured in other MUDs based on similar code, because it's a strange sense of deja vu to be traveling through the exact same landscape on several different MUDs. High-level characters are encouraged to write new areas for MR, so that the world is constantly expanding. These multiple authors all have different styles, and the locations are highly varied. The result is a mish-mash, of course, but it's a pleasant mish-mash. The variety this system provides is preferable to the alternative, a well-ordered yet dull game world. 

MR's VT102 interface is fairly unique. Instead of presenting the MUD as a single, scrolling page, the interface separates user input from game events, eliminating confusion. Also, the horizontal bars contain important information about your character, so you don't have to keep checking your stats to figure out how many hit points or movement points you have left. It's surprising that more MUDs don't use a similar interface: it's simple, but highly useful. 

Finally, the on-line help is excellent. Typing help brings you to a logically ordered menu system which provides information on a variety of topics. Most MUDs have some form of on-line help, but it's usually presented in little, hard-to-find chunks of information or a single, huge, poorly-organized document. The help feature on MR is a welcome change of pace. 

### Deities of the Realms

Mortal Realms is an ideal place for the beginner. Aside from the useful help feature, beginners in Realms are really protected from other characters. Nobody can bash you to pieces or steal all your gold the minute you walk into Chakkor (the starting city), and this is definitely a plus. 

Two gods exist in MR, Order and Chaos. They are the programmers and maintainers of MR, and they're both friendly and helpful (though they're usually pretty busy). A character may choose to become a follower of Order or Chaos, or to remain completely neutral. Followers of Order may kill or steal from followers of Chaos, and the reverse is true as well. Followers of the same god may not kill or steal from one another, and neutral characters are immune from both practices. 

This structure works very neatly. Players who are interested in player killing (and all the risks and rewards that go with it) will quickly make the decision to convert. Players who prefer to avoid the headaches and difficulties of player killing may choose to avoid the issue altogether. 

### Class System of the Realms

The Realms offer an interesting multiclass system, so that a player who starts out as one type of character can switch to another profession at every tenth experience level. Hence, an elementalist has a few of each type of spell, as long as they are based on the elements of the 4 planes. The Elementalist has the distinct advantage of being able to cast spells on creatures in an adjacent room. The Elementalist can wield only clubs, staffs, and whips. 

Manipulating your character's class is one of the most entertaining parts of the game. Should you stay with one profession for a long time, gaining mastery of one set of skills, or switch repeatedly and become a jack of all trades? Certain professions have weapon and armor restrictions, and this adds to the strategy. If your ranger becomes a necromancer, then he won't be able to use his sword any more! And there's a final reason to put some serious thought into your character's class: once you switch from one class to another, you can never re-enter the old profession! 

### Succeeding in the Realms

When you first start out on MR, the computer will generate an endless supply of new characters until you pick one you like. The statistics (strength, dexterity, constitution, intelligence and wisdom) are random, as is the race of your character. Don't worry about the race much, just look at the composite of statistics. The average composite of stats (a number provided for your convenience as each new character is rolled) is 13.0, but you want to keep rolling until you find a character with a composite of at least 14.4. The starting range of abilities in MR seems to be pretty narrow: fifteen minutes of fast rolling will turn up only a few characters with composites of 14.6. If you can get a character with a 14.8 composite, you've got a real find. 

The statistics which seem most useless are actually the most useful to a new character. Wisdom determines the number of advances your character will receive with each new level, while intelligence determines the number of advances necessary to pump up skills to their maximum values. Constitution determines the number of hit points received with each new level. The bottom line is, don't throw away your starting points on strength or dexterity advances, and definitely do not buy hit points or mana points. Work on strength, constitution and wisdom first, then build up the other stats. Then, when you have little else to improve, you can buy raw hit points and mana points. 

Don't be confused by the languages of MR. If people speak to you in gibberish, then they're speaking language you can't understand. The who command will list all the characters on-line, along with information about each character. From this list you can determine who is speaking what language. The speak command will let you shift to another language, hopefully one that other characters can grasp. Don't worry if you can't communicate with certain people at the start. Just gain a few levels and you'll learn new languages: also, the magic shops sell potions that will help you communicate and understand other characters. 

Think carefully about your character's class in MR. If you're going to play this game a lot, you might consider holding off on the ranger class until very late in your career. The ranger can wield almost all weapons and wear just about any armor. This is more of an asset than you might expect, and you'll surely regret it if you start as a ranger and then convert to another profession. Thinking back, you'll long for the good old days when you could wield or strap on anything you found. So think it through! 

This should be enough to get you started on MR. It's a fun MUD with a lot to do; the only pitfall is the permanency of your decisions. Changing class or pledging to one of the two on-line gods is a big decision, so beware. I'd suggest playing a test character for a while. Using this character, look at all the highest level characters and ask them what professions they adopted. After you've learned the ropes you'll be ready to make your permanent character. Good luck!
